Step-by-Step Cleaning Process (Detailed) 
1. Backup Original File 
What: Save an untouched copy of the raw dataset before any modification. 
Why: Prevents accidental data loss and provides a rollback point. 
How (Excel): File → Save As → Retail_Sales_Raw_backup.xlsx. Work on a copy named 
Retail_Sales_Work.xlsx. 
Benefit: Ensures reproducibility and auditability. 
2. Inspect (shape, head, missing %) 
What: Quick data profiling to understand size, sample records, data types and missingness. 
Why: Guides priorities for cleaning and sets expectations. 
How (Excel): Open sheet, use COUNTA/ROWS/COLUMNS or Excel Status Bar for count. Use 
conditional formatting and COUNTBLANK(range) per column. Use PivotTables to inspect 
categories and ranges. 
Benefit: Identifies problem columns early and estimates effort required. 
3. Standardize Column Names 
What: Rename columns consistently (snake_case or Title Case). 
Why: Clean names improve readability and avoid errors during automation, imports and 
scripting. 
How (Excel): Edit header row cells or use Power Query: Transform → Use First Row as 
Headers → Rename columns: order_id, order_date, ship_date, etc. 
Benefit: Consistent column naming reduces mistakes when writing formulas, DAX or code. 
4. Trim & Clean Text Columns 
What: Remove leading/trailing spaces and hidden non-printable characters from text fields. 
Why: Invisible characters cause false duplicates and grouping errors (e.g., 'Claire ' ≠ 
'Claire'). 
How (Excel formulas): 
 - Helper column formula: =TRIM(CLEAN(SUBSTITUTE(E2,CHAR(160),""))) 
 - Fill down and Paste Special → Values back into original column. 
 - Alternatively use Power Query: Transform → Format → Trim and Clean. 
Benefit: Ensures exact matches for joins and grouping; fixes Ctrl+G blank-detection issues. 
5. Parse Dates & Convert Numerics 
What: Convert date-like columns into Excel Date type and numeric columns into numbers. 
Why: Proper types are required for arithmetic, sorting, date functions, and charts. 
How (Excel): 
 - For mixed date formats: =DATEVALUE(SUBSTITUTE(A2,"-","/")) or use Power Query 
to change type using locale. 
 - For numerics stored as text: use VALUE(SUBSTITUTE(...)) or Text to Columns trick. 
Benefit: Enables accurate calculations (ship lag, month grouping, sums, averages). 
6. Flag Missing Values and Create Imputed Flag Columns 
What: For every column where imputation is possible, create a flag column that records if 
the value was originally missing. 
Why: Flags preserve provenance and transparency for clients and future reviewers. 
How (Excel): 
 - Add columns e.g., sales_missing_flag with formula: =IF(ISBLANK(Q2),1,0) 
 - Use COUNTIF on flags to log imputed counts into ChangeLog. 
Benefit: Maintains traceability and allows filtering to inspect imputed rows. 
7. Detect Duplicates and Mark (Don’t Delete Immediately) 
What: Identify exact duplicates and duplicates by business keys (e.g., order_id + product_id 
+ order_date). 
Why: Duplicates can distort aggregates; but some repeated order_ids can be legitimate (line 
items), so do not delete without inspection. 
How (Excel): 
 - Use Conditional Formatting → Duplicate Values for quick scan. 
 - Create a helper key column: =order_id & "|" & product_id & "|" & 
TEXT(order_date,"yyyy-mm-dd") and use COUNTIF(key_range,key_cell). 
 - Create duplicate_flag = IF(COUNTIF(key_range,key_cell)>1,1,0). 
Benefit: Ensures correct totals and removes accidental double-records only after manual 
review. 
8. Standardize Categories 
What: Normalize categorical column values (Ship_Mode, Category, Sub_Category, State, 
Postal_Code formatting). 
Why: Different spellings or formats split groups and misrepresent group totals. 
How (Excel): 
 - Use Find & Replace to correct common variants (e.g., 'Std' -> 'Standard Class'). 
 - Postal code correction: use =TEXT(L2,"000000") for 6-digit standardization or manual 
correction based on city/state. 
Benefit: Ensures accurate grouping and that maps/pivots aggregate correctly. 
9. Validate Business Rules 
What: Validate logical constraints such as Ship_Date >= Order_Date and Sales ≥ 0. 
Why: Ensures records reflect real-world processes and detects entry errors. 
How (Excel): 
 - Add helper: ship_lag_days = =[@Ship_Date]-[@Order_Date], then filter ship_lag_days < 
0. 
 - Use formulas: =IF(OR(Sales<=0,Quantity<=0),"Check","OK"). 
Benefit: Catches impossible or suspicious records for correction before analysis. 
10. Handle Missing Values and Impute Conservatively 
What: Apply conservative, explainable imputation rules and record them. 
Why: Preserves dataset size for analysis while maintaining transparency. 
How (Imputation rules used in this project): 
 - Customer_ID: fill with 'Unknown' 
 - Product_ID: fill with 'MissingProduct' 
 - Discount: fill with 0 
 - Sales: fill with MEDIAN(sales_range) using =MEDIAN(Q2:Q1501) 
 - Quantity: fill with ROUND(MEDIAN(quantity_range),0) 
 - Profit: compute median profit margin = MEDIAN(IF(sales<>0,profit/sales)), then profit 
= median_margin * sales for missing profits 
 - Ship_Date: impute as Order_Date + median shipping lag (calculated per ship_mode if 
available), and flag ship_date_imputed_flag 
 
Excel mechanics: 
 - Use Ctrl+G → Special → Blanks to select blanks and paste formulas like =$AE$1 
(median) then Paste Special → Values. 
 - Create imputation flag columns before filling: e.g., sales_imputed_flag = 
IF(ISBLANK(Q2),1,0). 
Benefit: Imputation provides plausible values while preserving ability to filter and audit 
imputed rows. 
11. Detect & Flag Outliers 
What: Identify profit outliers using the IQR method and flag them for review. 
Why: Outliers can distort averages and model training. 
How (Excel): 
 - Calculate Q1 and Q3: =QUARTILE.INC(T2:T1501,1) and =QUARTILE.INC(T2:T1501,3) 
 - IQR = Q3 - Q1. Bounds: Lower = Q1 - 1.5*IQR, Upper = Q3 + 1.5*IQR 
 - Flag: =IF(OR(T2<Lower,T2>Upper),"Outlier","Normal"). 
Benefit: Enables focused inspection and informed decisions (cap, remove or retain) on 
extreme profit values. 
12. Create Derived Columns (Feature Engineering) 
What: Add new columns that make analysis easier and richer. 
Why: Derived features support segmentation, trend analysis and advanced visuals. 
How (Excel formulas examples): 
 - order_year: =YEAR(order_date) 
 - order_month: =TEXT(order_date,"yyyy-mm") 
 - processing_days: =ship_date - order_date 
 - profit_margin: =IF(sales=0,NA(),profit/sales) 
 - sales_per_unit: =sales/quantity 
 - discount_band: =IF(discount=0,"0%",IF(discount<=0.1,"1-10%",IF(discount<=0.2,"11
20%","21%+"))) 
 - high_value_flag: =IF(sales>5000,"High","Regular") 
Benefit: Feature columns speed up pivoting, filtering and plotting; they are inputs to 
dashboards and models. 
13. Reconcile Totals and Sanity-Check Results 
What: Compare sums and counts before and after cleaning and ensure consistency. 
Why: Confirms no accidental row deletion and verifies aggregates are within expected 
ranges. 
How (Excel): 
 - Compare SUM(Sales) before vs after and verify Orders count = 1500. 
 - Use PivotTables to compare totals by Month/Region and spot unexpected changes. 
Benefit: Validates cleaning work and builds confidence in downstream analysis. 
14. Save Cleaned File and Document Steps 
What: Save final cleaned workbook and record all changes in a ChangeLog sheet. 
Why: Ensures reproducibility, audit trail and client-ready deliverable. 
How (Excel): 
 - Save as Retail_Sales_Clean_v1.xlsx and include a sheet named ChangeLog with columns 
Date, Step, Column, MissingBefore, Method, ImputedCount, Notes. 
 - Keep flags and derived columns in the file for transparency. 
Benefit: Final deliverable is ready for Power BI dashboarding and modeling; client can 
review the change log. 
 
